Use two pointers, recording overlaps including touching endpoints, then advance whichever interval ends first. Neither input is modified.

```javascript
function solve(a, b) {
  const result = [];
  let i = 0;
  let j = 0;

  while (i < a.length && j < b.length) {
    const start = Math.max(a[i][0], b[j][0]);
    const end = Math.min(a[i][1], b[j][1]);

    if (start <= end) {
      result.push([start, end]);
    }

    if (a[i][1] < b[j][1]) {
      i++;
    } else if (a[i][1] > b[j][1]) {
      j++;
    } else {
      i++;
      j++;
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

Time: O(a.length + b.length). Auxiliary space: O(1), excluding O(k) for the returned intersections. Call solve(a, b) directly with two sorted lists of disjoint closed intervals; no imports or exports are needed.

